# Java Access Modifiers

Access modifiers in Java control **where a class, variable, method, or constructor can be accessed from**.

In simple words:

> **Access modifiers decide who is allowed to access something in your Java code.**

Java has four access levels:

```text
public
protected
default
private
```

---

# 1. Why Do We Need Access Modifiers?

Suppose we have:

```java
class Student {

    String name;
    int marks;
}
```

Anyone who can access the `Student` object may be able to access these variables.

But sometimes we don't want everything to be accessible everywhere.

For example:

```text
Student
 ├── name       → maybe public
 ├── marks      → maybe private
 └── password   → definitely private
```

Access modifiers help us control this.

---

# 2. `public`

`public` means:

> **Accessible from anywhere where the class itself is accessible.**

Example:

```java
public class Student {

    public String name;

    public void display() {
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}
```

Another class can access it:

```java
Student student = new Student();

student.name = "Nikitha";
student.display();
```

---

# 3. `private`

`private` means:

> **Accessible only inside the same class.**

Example:

```java
class Student {

    private int marks;

    void setMarks(int marks) {
        this.marks = marks;
    }

    int getMarks() {
        return marks;
    }
}
```

Now this is not allowed:

```java
Student student = new Student();

student.marks = 90;
```

Because `marks` is private.

Instead, we use methods:

```java
student.setMarks(90);

System.out.println(student.getMarks());
```

This is one of the basic ideas behind **encapsulation**.

---

# 4. `default`

When we don't write any access modifier, Java gives the member **default/package-private access**.

Example:

```java
class Student {

    String name;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}
```

Here, `name` and `display()` have default access.

They can be accessed by classes in the **same package**.

But classes from a different package cannot directly access them.

---

# 5. `protected`

`protected` allows access:

- Inside the same class
- Inside the same package
- In subclasses, including subclasses in another package

Example:

```java
class Student {

    protected String name;
}
```

A subclass can access it:

```java
class CollegeStudent extends Student {

    void display() {
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}
```

Here `CollegeStudent` inherits from `Student`, so it can access the protected member.

---

# 6. Simple Comparison

| Modifier | Same Class | Same Package | Subclass | Other Package |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `private` | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |
| `default` | ✅ | ✅ | ✅* | ❌ |
| `protected` | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | Limited |
| `public` | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |

`*` A subclass in the same package can access default members because it is also in the same package.

For `protected`, a subclass in another package can access the protected member through inheritance.

---

# 7. Easy Way to Remember

Think of a college:

```text
private
↓
Only me

default
↓
People in my package

protected
↓
My package + my child classes

public
↓
Everyone
```

